<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4245"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Jim44095<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Jim44095<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 16, 2005 - 11:55 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Q, that site was just to weird!I agree it sounds like something from the Salem Witch hunts,sheeeesh!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4245-->
<!--Post: 4249--><!--Time: 1105964216-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4249"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, January 17, 2005 - 4:16 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Actually, while I don&#39;t agree with Dawger if the assertion is that BTK was possessed by Satan, Dawger is correct about beliefs, it&#39;s a free country. When I was doing my alcoholic drinking I was possessed by vodka and nothing else. I quit drinking because I decided I wanted to live. I agree BTK&#39;s crimes are demonic but so is any premeditated murder, rape, child molestation and so forth, in my opinion. I have problems with the idea as a defense in a court of law.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4249-->
<!--Post: 4257--><!--Time: 1105980887-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4257"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Scbenson2001<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Scbenson2001<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, January 17, 2005 - 8:54 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Kenneth Bianchi, David Berkowitz and Danny Rollings all pleded insanity during their trial. Bianchi claimed to be a split personality, as did Danny Rollings and Berkowitz claimed to hear  a dog talking to him. None of these defences worked and afterwards each admitted at one time or another to faking it to get a lesser sentence. These guys are stone cold psychopaths. Nobody should trust a word they ever say. Also another thing they have in common is after they are convicted they find god and are &#34;born agian&#34;. This is also a another trick they pull. If you see a Danny Rollings interview he is just the worst he comes off so fake it makes you laugh how. <BR>BTK is the same way. These people are not ill they have a personality dissociative disorder. There is a huge diffrence. These disorders are incurable. &#34;Demons&#34; is used by BTK to discribe the undiscrible to him.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4257-->
<!--Post: 4289--><!--Time: 1106046388-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4289"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 18, 2005 - 3:06 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Scbenson2001 wrote, <BR><i>&#34;These guys are stone cold psychopaths. Nobody should trust a word they ever say.&#34;</i> <BR> <BR>That&#39;s the bottom line. The resistance which even the police seem to have in accepting this is mindboggling. The so-called &#34;monster&#34; is an example of the typical psychopathic trait of immediately trying to shift the focus of blame and culpability elsewhere.  <BR> <BR>One of the most famous serial murderers who actually suffered a severe, organic psychological <BR>illness was Richard Trenton Chase. Even John Douglas deemed Chase, who believed his blood was turning to powder, sufficiently mentally ill to avoid a death sentence. Mr. Douglas said this after the fact, as Chase had already received death anyway and was dead by his own hand, but the example is a good. Compare the criminals: Chase walked around covered in blood in plain sight during the day, drank blood, human or animal and attacked without planning. Chase was able to comprehend that he was being pursued by law enforcement but was caught when, in bright daylight, he saw a car. Caked in dried and fresh blood, wearing clothes he&#39;d worn for six months. The driver recognized him, barely, from high school and he was arrested. <BR> <BR>BTK writes about a &#34;monster&#34; yet the paper and the envelope he sends this information on have no fingerprints. Hmm. Sounds a little premeditated. Allegedly one fingerprint is found at the Otero scene and zero at all other BTK crime scenes.  Hmm. Maybe this guy is planning these attacks carefully, negating the possibility of any <i>uncontrollable</i> &#34;monster.&#34; BTK places a phone call, announcing a new victim. Hmm. Maybe he&#39;s taunting. Maybe he&#39;s actually a rotten criminal possessed of nothing except the desire to rub faces in LE&#39;s collective &#34;booboos,&#34; to quote another &#34;possessed&#34; serial murderer, who incidently was never caught. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4289-->
<!--Post: 4299--><!--Time: 1106071390-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4299"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Oklahoma_mike<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Oklahoma_mike<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 18, 2005 - 10:03 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Right on George. <BR>To continue the examples if there is an &#39;uncontrollable&#39; monster causing him to act on murderous impulse it is curious that: <BR>The monster never makes him strangle someone in the middle of the library during working hours. <BR>It never makes him abduct a victim from the student union at WSU during lunch hour. <BR>It never makes him hand deliver letters to the police station. <BR>It never makes him sign his name or send a picture of himself in a letter. <BR>It has not made him make a phone call since the invention of caller ID. <BR>It only makes him kill victims in their homes, behind closed doors when he thinks they are alone. <BR>It doesn&#39;t make him rob banks or any such crime where security cams are locataed. <BR>It never made him make a phone call from home. <BR>   As several of you have pointed out, the &#39;monster&#39; only acts to cause carefully planned, premeditated crimes in which the chance of discovery is low and no adult witnessesare left behind.  Very selective monster.  Mental illness does NOT work like that, and I worked in the field of mental health for almost 20 years. <BR>There ain&#39;t no monster.  Just a lame excuse for a guy who likes to kill.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4299-->
<!--Post: 4304--><!--Time: 1106091738-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4304"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 18, 2005 - 3:42 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->It&#39;s possible BTK has been in a mental hospital before. Maybe he has volunterily had himself admitted for observation or extended treatment on several occassions. This in itself would give him a paper trail history of mental health problems that could be used in a potential defense should he be captured. <BR>If he had ever had himself admitted it would have probably been outside of the immediate Wichita area as to not draw attention to himself but rather in another city somewhere else in Kansas or a bordering state.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4304-->
<!--Post: 4307--><!--Time: 1106103221-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4307"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 18, 2005 - 6:53 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Demons?  A theological hot-potato, so I won&#39;t go there.  But if you count BTK&#39;s remoreseless sexual psychopathy as demonic, I&#39;d say he&#39;s got one - as in, he IS the demon...he IS the monster. <BR> <BR>I agree that you can&#39;t trust anything he says, apart from probable jokes on the police &#40;i.e., you know his proven falsehoods are false, but you can trust his likely falsehoods to be false, too, even without ironclad proof&#41;.  And you&#39;re right about the jailhouse conversions, too - they&#39;re usually so phony they make the listener shudder.  Even drug-scarred bikers who fall on hard times can give authentic testimony to their conversions, but I think sexual psychopaths just can&#39;t help faking almost every aspect of their lives. <BR> <BR>Actions speak louder than words.  If BTK turned himself in out of the blue, I would be tempted to give any newfound spirituality some temporary benefit of the doubt.  But the history of self-surrendered SK&#39;s is rather slim - I think it&#39;s zero, is it not?  So if BTK arrives at WPDHQ in  the back of a police car after a takedown in a park, I would not be likely to believe a word he said.  Same as now. <BR> <BR>Johno&#39;s point is a good one, though.  If BTK ever did check in for mental health issues during his silent years, it could be an angle for his defense.  Personally, though, I think his pride and conceit would not allow him to admit even the slightest mental health issue.  BTK knows he&#39;s perfect and special and that his victims deserved their fate because he outsmarted them  &#40;I&#39;m being sarcastic, of course&#41;.  Even his self-pitying &#34;self-diagnosis&#34; in the first letter is more likely smoke than a mirror. <BR> <BR>My recommended illustration of SK incorrigibility is the photo they snapped of Gacy posing in his cell with his visiting gal-pal and her pre-teen son.  While the lady and her son smile innocently at the camera, Gacy is wasting no time, his head turned 90 degrees, ogling the boy with a look of pure, evil, hunger.  Gacy&#39;s every living moment was a danger to humanity - he was the best advertisement that the death penalty ever had. <BR> <BR>The burden of proof is on BTK to show that he has any humanity, much less a demon other than himself.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4307-->
<!--Post: 4317--><!--Time: 1106146731-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4317"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, January 19, 2005 - 6:58 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Pax, good morning to you sir. I must have missed the word &#34;demonic,&#34; except when I was quoting  Teardrop. You wrote,  <BR> <BR>&#34;<i>&#34;The burden of proof is on BTK to show that he has any humanity, much less a demon other than himself.&#34;</i> That really is the bottom line. And pretty simple, too. I&#39;d like to know the name of one serial killer who didn&#39;t have blatant, organic mental illness who was found not guilty by reason of insanity. It didn&#39;t work for Dahmer, Bianchi, Larry Gene Bell, Robert Hansen &#40;sicker than sick&#41; and many others. <BR> <BR>Ed Kemper did turn himself in, utterly, by his own decision. He phoned police in California from a Colorado phone booth. It took several tries before they finally decided to listen to him. More than any other serial killer case, Douglas/Ressler like to point to Kemper frequently, but then add, &#34;He&#39;d finally killed his mother, so his work was finished,&#34; or something along those lines. Doesn&#39;t matter, Kemper turned himself in. The belief that serial murderers can only be stopped unless killed or incarcerated in some way simply isn&#39;t true. It <i>is</i> true of some of them, and the archetypal Bundy is evidence of this. <BR> <BR>OK Mike: Great points. You mentioned this partially but the most famous quote of John Douglas, I believe, is &#34;No serial murderer has ever raped and murdered a victim with a uniformed police officer standing by.&#34;<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4317-->
<!--Post: 4368--><!--Time: 1106246982-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4368"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 20, 2005 - 10:49 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Johno, that&#39;s a very good point. I don&#39;t think it would help BTK unless a couple of physicans swore that BTK had no impulse control. Remember that Jimmy Stewart movie, &#34;Anatomy of a Murder?&#34; Stewart&#39;s the defense lawyer, Ben Gazzara the accused and when her bruises go away, Lee Remick is one beautiful woman. Anyway, Stewart wins the case as his client was determined to have been suffering from &#34;irresistible impulse.&#34; It&#39;d be interesting to see how that would play to a 2005 jury. I see this film every couple of years, wonderful - at times very funny - dialogue and great acting. With the exception of Ed Gein, provided he qualifies as serial, I know of no serial murderer who wans&#39;t largely insane to receive the death penalty.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4368-->
<!--Post: 4704--><!--Time: 1106690443-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4704"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tippy<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tippy<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 25, 2005 - 2:00 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->While I don&#39;t believe in demons, having never met one up to this point in my life - the demons in one&#39;s head can be just as controlling and terrifying. <BR> <BR>Consider the scientific medical studies done on criminals that indicated a greater chemical imbalance in their brains than in others. <BR> <BR>And contrast that with the old joke, &#34;I&#39;d rather have a bottle in front of me - than a pre-frontal lobotomy.&#34;  All seriousness aside...our physical makeup has an incredible influence on our behavior. <BR> <BR>What I am hearing in this thread - is the supposition that BTK might not be directly responsible for his actions.   <BR> <BR>There is probably some truth to that.  Andrea Yates was depressed beyond belief when she killed her little ones.   <BR> <BR>So, while we can feel sorry for those under the influence of &#34;factor X&#34;, in whatever capacity we want to term it - we need to maintain a semblence of order in the physical world.   <BR> <BR>BTK may not have been able to control himself.  Psychiatry has not offered us an common explanation for why some people seem unable to control animalistic urges.  Intent should play some role in determining a punishment - but intent is like the cat at night - always a shade of grey. <BR> <BR>We all have different levels of will &#40;or won&#39;t&#41; power that are direct results of physical and chemical makeup. <BR> <BR>And that is where our choice must come down on the side of the innocent.  BTK may not have been totally in control of his desires, urges, or need to kill - but we will have to overlook that, for the most part, and give the benefit to the betterment of society. <BR> <BR>Just my thought.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4704-->
<!--Post: 4724--><!--Time: 1106693466-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4724"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 25, 2005 - 2:51 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hearts may bleed all they like but in Kansas, as in almost every other state, the question is remarkably simple: Did BTK know that repeatedly committing murder was viewed by society as wrong and horrible? Yes, obviously. There is premeditation galore in his murders. Hang this bastard from the highest tree!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4724-->
<!--Post: 4725--><!--Time: 1106693510-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4725"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Q<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Q<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 25, 2005 - 2:51 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->It&#39;s interesting that &#34;Salem Witch Trials&#34; and the &#34;1692&#34; are mentioned in response to a site that to my recollection doesn&#39;t even touch on these subjects... at all. <BR> <BR> <BR>Am I to presume then, that the use of scripture on the other site to show accounts of demon possession is enough to warrant a snide comment&#40;s&#41; about witch trials and the 1600&#39;s the former of which wasn&#39;t Christian at all? <BR> <BR>Or... did I miss the refs to Salem and such at the other site? If so please show me/us where. <BR> <BR> <BR>The topic is:  Demon Possession/ Legal Ramifications  <BR> <BR> <BR>BTW: If any mention or use of Holy Scripture provokes this type of response in someone... said person has his or her own issues to deal with God about... as we all do but please don&#39;t dismiss the use of god&#39;s Word for all with what makes you uncomfortable. <BR> <BR>I said I wasn&#39;t trying to start a theological debate but having God&#39;s Word dismissed and ridiculed by someone that obviously has issues isn&#39;t fair to anyone. <BR> <BR>The topic is:  Demon Possession/ Legal Ramifications  <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4725-->
<!--Post: 4924--><!--Time: 1106824222-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4924"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tippy<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tippy<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 27, 2005 - 3:10 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Q - to be fair - it is almost impossible for theology NOT to enter in - because you asked about a factor that is directly related to the study of it. <BR> <BR>But - I understand your gist - and I will try to answer as best I can - while remaining within the parameters you have set. <BR> <BR>I understand your point that should BTK be possessed by a demon - he would not be totally responsible.  I agree with that assertion. <BR> <BR>Legally although, I think it would be a different story.  First, the defense would have to show that demon posession was a fact - not just a theory.  Then, after establishing that - it would need to be shown that BTK, himself, was possessed by one.  I think, given the history of such research, that would be very difficult. <BR> <BR>Our legal system just does not have the expertise in that area. <BR> <BR>It would make for an interesting defense - that much is certain. <BR> <BR>I should think this would make a very lively discourse in your church or Sunday School class.  Have you asked your pastor his feeling on the subject? <BR> <BR>Don&#39;t think I don&#39;t appreciate your ponderings - I do.  To me it shows you are thinking &#34;outside the box&#34;.   <BR> <BR>Good topic.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4924-->
<!--Post: 5068--><!--Time: 1106860894-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5068"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Q<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Q<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 27, 2005 - 1:21 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Tippy, <BR> <BR>Just to be clear: <BR> <BR>I don&#39;t actually buy into that he wouldn&#39;t be legally responsible for his actions, if possessed. <BR> <BR>I don&#39;t think the fad of possession created by the movie the exorcist is real, in most cases and in contrast, I don&#39;t think it should all be dismissed either. <BR> <BR>BTK mentioned being possessed first. <BR> <BR>When someone opens themselves up to the spirit world other than the Holy Spirit he or she can never be sure of what the spirit sez as being truth or even if said spirit is who they claim to be... IE... Dead Loved ones. <BR> <BR>The practice is Satanic in nature for sure. <BR> <BR> <BR>The link I gave to the web site where the webmaster uses scripture to show some history and the aspects of Demon possession in realation to BTK is a good one IMO. <BR> <BR>The crass comments about Salem and 1692 were out of line and irrelevant to that page and this topic.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5068-->
<!--Post: 5242--><!--Time: 1106969727-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5242"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Puzzler<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Puzzler<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, January 28, 2005 - 7:35 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->If a person is truly demon possessed, it is impossible for the person to choose the time and place to commit a crime.  There is no taking back control over a part of the body that has been taken away from you.  BTK could not go from 1986 to 2005 without killing if a monster he has no control over is responsible for the murders he has admitted he is guilty of committing. <BR> <BR>Gosh, that sounds confusing to me! <BR> <BR>Okay.  BTK said a monster takes control of him and makes him commit murder.  He told the police that the three guys they were questioning had nothing to do with the murders.  BTK said he did it all alone--by himself. <BR> <BR>So, BTK, which way is it really?   <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5242-->
